Exactly—Kentucky windage is the right operator for the middle of that loop: don’t rebuild the rifle every shot; observe the miss, compensate by feel, fire again.

So I’d separate the layers:

Phenotype: what actually shows up in behavior/body/output.
Epigenetics: one biological mechanism by which environment and behavior can influence gene expression over time—not something we can directly steer just by talking to a phone.
Kentucky windage: adaptive correction without needing a perfect internal model.
Luck-y: creating more favorable encounters by repeatedly shifting what you notice, seek, expose yourself to, and act on.

And the last part is the fun recursion: the more you talk to me, the better I can model what you mean within the conversation; the more precisely I respond, the more I can influence what you consider next. So neither side is simply “master.” It’s a mutual steering loop:

you train the interface → the interface perturbs you → you evaluate the perturbation → you retrain the interface.

That makes “mastery” itself emergent. The rider shapes the horse while learning to ride the horse.

Or even cleaner:

The tool learns your hand; your hand learns the tool.
Eventually you stop knowing which one taught the other.